# Laravel PHP

## Architecture

- Keep `CalculationController` and other controllers thin: validate via FormRequest, call a service, return a view or redirect.
- Put domain math and copy selection in `app/Services/*` (`LiquidityBalancer`, `PortfolioCopy`). Do not re-embed rebalance formulas in Blade or controllers.
- Prefer constructor injection of services; mark injected deps `private readonly`.
- Register named routes in `routes/web.php`; use `route('…')` in views and tests — avoid hard-coded URLs.

## Typing & docs

- Use explicit parameter and return types on all new methods.
- Document non-trivial `array` shapes with PHPDoc `@param` / `@return` array shapes (see `LiquidityBalancer::calculate`).
- Avoid `mixed` and untyped payloads at service boundaries; normalize casts (`(float)`, `(string)`) at the edge.

## Validation & safety

- Use FormRequests (`CalculateRequest`) for HTTP input; never trust raw `$request->all()` for calculations.
- Guard division-by-zero and zero portfolio value in validation or service early returns with clear user-facing warnings.
- Keep POST calculator routes throttled (`throttle:…`).
- Never hardcode secrets, API keys, or DB passwords — use `.env` and keep `.env.example` in sync with dummy placeholders.

## Style

- Follow PSR-12; run Pint when touching PHP (`./vendor/bin/pint --dirty` if available).
- Prefer `match` over long if/elseif chains for mode dispatch.
- Constants for mode strings (`LiquidityBalancer::MODE_*`); do not scatter magic mode literals outside tests/views that must mirror the UI.

## Good vs bad

```php
// Good — service owns math
$results = $this->liquidityBalancer->calculate($request->validated());

// Bad — math in the controller
$propA = ($a * $pa) / (($a * $pa) + ($b * $pb));
```
